WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.649
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Reece Peck, who's an associate professor

00:00:03.649 --> 00:00:10.420
at the City University of New York and also
author of the book Fox Populism Branding Conservatism

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.500
as Working Class.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:14.660
Really appreciate your time and for being
here today.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:16.390
Speaker 5: Yeah, my pleasure.

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:23.070
Speaker 1: So to start with, either my my
understanding of the history of political

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:29.029
media, including sort of as described by the
book Waves of Rancor, which starts with the

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:36.730
use of low power F.M. and talk radio and sort
of all the way through where we are now is

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:41.980
that the political right and the political
left have used media differently.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:46.410
Part of that has to do with ownership structure
and issues related to money and financing.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:51.720
But part of it also has to do with the right,
quite frankly, having better, I would argue,

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:56.230
more effective sort of bumper sticker phrases
that better lend themselves to some forms

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:57.460
of media.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:01.370
Can you talk a little bit about the current
landscape and how you would sort of distinguish

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:04.159
the way the political right and left use media?

00:01:04.159 --> 00:01:09.990
Speaker 5: Yeah, well, first off, the right,
it's hard to talk about the kind of right

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:11.290
versus left media.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:17.159
You really have to think about a kind of liberal
corporatist center that dominates the mainstream

00:01:17.159 --> 00:01:22.369
legacy media and then this massive right wing
sector that's always been there for multiple

00:01:22.369 --> 00:01:27.680
generations, and then this little sliver of
truly leftist media and alternative spaces.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.020
So you kind of have to break it down in a
tripartite model.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:38.360
It's harder to talk about like left media
versus liberal centrist media, but the left

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:43.380
media, in terms of the history of alternative
left media, I mean, today there's a common

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.280
sense notion that the mainstream media is
liberal, has a liberal bias.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:50.920
But if you took a time machine to the 1930s
when they talked about the bias of the press,

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:54.390
it was always the capitalist press or the
conservative press.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:59.799
And so labor unions and lefties had to create
these alternative revenue models, had to kind

00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:02.710
of fight their way to get on radio.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:04.619
And it's always been this uphill, uphill battle.

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:09.440
I mean, if you're critical of capitalism and
the media system you exist in, it's commercial

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.930
in nature, right?

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:15.390
It's an uphill battle for funding for legitimacy.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:20.430
And so that's largely the history of the left,
like the true left kind of alternative media,

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:21.730
the conservative media.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:28.519
Inversely, I guess you'd say when you have
this pro-business, class, free market ideology,

00:02:28.519 --> 00:02:30.120
the ways that you're back, right?

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.750
You're basically telling media executives
and billionaire donors that they're the moral

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:35.750
history.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:38.190
I mean, they're the moral heroes, they're
the job creators.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:41.819
So they're going to look kindly on you and
your in your content.

00:02:41.819 --> 00:02:46.000
And so but I don't think that structural analysis,
it can only go so far.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.800
And I think leftists throw their hands in
the air and they say, well, we don't have

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.080
the same billionaire backing, we don't have
the same funding.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.700
And I think you're very right to say there
are things in which left media creators do

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:01.349
to tie their own hands, to shoot themselves
in the foot, in terms of messaging, in terms

00:03:01.349 --> 00:03:03.560
of style, in terms of narrative.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:07.910
That that has really catapulted the right
is I completely agree with you.

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:11.450
They're kind of more sophisticated in terms
of their communication and media strategies.

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:14.970
Speaker 1: Can you talk a little, give some
examples of that?

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:19.849
And, you know, I mean, when I talk to others
in the left media space and when I talk about

00:03:19.849 --> 00:03:22.320
that, just to be really clear, I'm not talking
about MSNBC.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:27.590
MSNBC is sort of like a Democratic Party center,
left positioning corporate profit motives

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:31.760
that are identical to Fox News, etc., even
if some of their policies are different, certainly

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.450
their editorial perspective is different.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:38.680
But when I talk to other people in like left
independent media, there are many who say

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:44.350
I would gladly accept the millionaire or even
billionaire donors of The Daily Wire if they

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:49.989
came to me and said, Hey, I want to give you
money to do exactly what you're doing.

00:03:49.989 --> 00:03:53.099
The counterpoint to that is those who say,
No, no, no, no, no.

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:59.380
By the very nature of having those millionaires
or billionaires supporting you, you would

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:01.510
not be able to do what you're doing.

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:05.870
That's the difference between what the left
wants to espouse and what the right wants

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:06.870
to espouse.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:14.829
So how much of it is that the messaging of
the left is not appealing to those moneyed

00:04:14.829 --> 00:04:17.739
interests versus something else?

00:04:17.739 --> 00:04:23.680
Speaker 5: Well, I would say that there was
no shortage of millionaire billionaire money

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:29.400
for right wing media in the 1950s and sixties
seventies, but they were not nearly as successful.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.790
And so it was all money and donations.

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:38.139
And how come William Buckley wasn't, you know,
a kind of populist tabloid hit, or how come

00:04:38.139 --> 00:04:42.660
the first generation of conservatives were
so ineffective, their media before Limbaugh,

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:45.100
before Fox News, was largely on the margins.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:49.310
It was a boutique kind of segment of the conservative
movement.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:54.180
It was only when you get to Limbaugh where
you see this blend of kind of profitability.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:59.169
They were they're a profitable commercial
entertainment product and then conservative

00:04:59.169 --> 00:05:00.520
ideology.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:05.169
And it's when the conservatives really, with
Murdoch's influence, embrace his tabloid style

00:05:05.169 --> 00:05:10.479
and populist style and integrated insights
from tabloid the public affairs genres like

00:05:10.479 --> 00:05:16.720
talk radio or tabloid television, that the
right attain this kind of level of cultural

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:18.360
dominance and prevalence that it did.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:23.320
And so I think there's lessons to be learned
from the left, right, the left has this inflexible

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:30.199
commitment to this kind of upper middle class,
educated, imagined audience in mind when they're

00:05:30.199 --> 00:05:34.370
when they're designing their style or they're
designing their kind of consumer appeals and

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:40.449
they're averse to this tabloid esthetic, they're
averse to kind of populist modes of engagement.

00:05:40.449 --> 00:05:45.880
And I think that's, you know, the success
of the right is largely a story of them shifting

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:51.490
toward a different style that's more conducive
to mass taste and kind of working class identity.

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:57.180
Speaker 1: So going back, like, what are the
stylistic mistakes that you think the left

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:00.290
is making that limit the appeal?

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:06.910
Speaker 5: Well, I think the left in especially
when, you know, there was this coming to Jesus

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:11.840
moment when George W Bush won his second term.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:15.889
And that was when you see this explosion of
kind of anti Fox Media ventures.

00:06:15.889 --> 00:06:17.290
We got to do something about Fox.

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:19.880
You get you know, Air America is launched.

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:25.310
You know, you get Jon Stewart kind of coming
into his own and critiquing and then you get

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:26.650
a, you know.

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:27.920
OLBERMANN a little later.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:33.319
And so there's this moment where they're like,
we need an unapologetic kind of form of progressive

00:06:33.319 --> 00:06:35.750
media to counter what Fox is doing.

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:41.980
I think my critique there is they went all
in on sarcasm and comedy in this kind of ironic

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:42.980
style.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:44.849
That was the way they were going to be popular
or appealing.

00:06:44.849 --> 00:06:46.189
And that has its uses.

00:06:46.189 --> 00:06:49.680
And we all like to a degree to satirical style.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:54.900
But I think it's limited when you have other
ways you can speak to people with sincerity,

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:59.360
with emotional expressiveness, with recruiting
certain personalities that don't come from

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:02.259
prestige sectors of the journalism field.

00:07:02.259 --> 00:07:04.240
And that's exactly what Fox did.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:09.470
MSNBC had a slight moment of flirtation with
populist styled hosts, with Jake Sugar and

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:11.280
Dylan Ratigan and Ed Schultz.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:13.300
And those shows did good.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:15.760
They were they had pretty good ratings.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:19.750
But it was I would say it's because of their
middlebrow taste culture that it was off brand

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:20.780
with MSNBC.

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:26.030
They were trying to appeal to this educated,
affluent demographic and they didn't want

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:32.250
to, you know, alienate them with this populist
sensibility.

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:35.240
And so I think that that that's the key to
me.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:41.400
And every time the last kind of plays with
this, they generally abandon those kind of

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:42.910
hosts, those kind of styles.

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:52.120
Speaker 1: George Lakoff has a perspective
where, media aside, he argues that some of

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:58.590
the arguments of the right are just structurally
better suited to commercial media.

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:03.159
Like a couple examples would be, Hey, abortion's
murder, so it should be banned.

00:08:03.159 --> 00:08:07.669
And on the left, it's like, well, you know,
we've got to talk about viability.

00:08:07.669 --> 00:08:14.420
And then you have to understand sort of like
the 40 week pregnancy and mammalian gestation.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:21.620
And we have to it's not like a good bumper
sticker phrase or, hey, the taxes, it's my

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:22.620
money.

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.080
Let me keep my money.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:30.660
Whereas the left is like, well, yes, one society's
get large enough, some roles need to be centralized.

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:33.149
And so we have to find the right tact, right?

00:08:33.149 --> 00:08:34.149
It's like.

00:08:34.149 --> 00:08:38.310
So he makes a more linguistic sort, that sort
of approach it.

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:44.260
How how important is that versus like strategic
media decisions that are made by left and

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:45.260
right?

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:47.000
Speaker 5: I think it's critical.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:48.830
You know, I'm not, you know.

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:53.779
LAKOFF The insights of Lakoff is that the
first way we interface with politics is often

00:08:53.779 --> 00:08:55.210
through these moral registers.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:09:00.339
It's not through these, like, nuanced policy
discussions or what the facts are.

00:09:00.339 --> 00:09:04.770
We really start with esthetics and moral frames
and then we move to the policy.

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:06.640
Though it's not that those things don't matter.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:10.570
It's just the way in which people interface
and the sequencing that they kind of come

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:13.330
to engage in politics through.

00:09:13.330 --> 00:09:14.500
And and I and I agree.

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:18.450
I mean, there's other civil rights leaders
like Reverend Barber, he's excellent.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:21.940
He's he's been calling for the progressives
to develop what he calls a kind of universal

00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:22.940
moral language.

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:28.529
And it doesn't mean when you speak in these
moral terms, you have to abandon nuance or

00:09:28.529 --> 00:09:29.529
complexity.

00:09:29.529 --> 00:09:33.870
It's just to recognize that that is engaging
from the from up front, Right.

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:39.820
That you're really laying out these kind of
positions in the right is really good at recognizing

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:44.640
not just speaking in a moralistic way, but
recognizing what moral political stories have

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:46.220
had resonance over time.

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:50.690
They're very good at looking at, like you
just mentioned, this idea that I work hard

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:52.810
and I deserve the fruits of my labor, right?

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:55.470
There's there's a way you could frame that
in a left wing way.

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:56.470
Yeah.

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:59.540
The labor movement works hard and they're
the ones who create wealth, right?

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:05.150
You could use that same moral frame like FDR
did, but the right captures that co-ops and

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:10.519
then turns that into a story about the fruits
of labor is really giving these business people

00:10:10.519 --> 00:10:12.700
and the job creators like all their hard work.

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:17.340
And so we need to not attack them because
they're the most productive segments of society.

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:22.570
So the right is like very savvy at recognizing
which narratives have resonated and cycled

00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:27.620
over political history and where the left
kind of shows these things and what the name

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:32.760
of intellectual complexity or that it's too
hokey or corny.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:34.870
And I just think that again, that's another
way.

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:37.290
And yet they're shooting themselves in the
foot.

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:43.920
Speaker 1: What do you make strategically
about the recent focus from the Republican

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:48.430
Party in media on the culture war stuff?

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:50.570
Anti-woke the trans issues?

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:55.279
You know, men in women's sports, as they like
to call it, etc..

00:10:55.279 --> 00:11:01.829
You know, a lot of the rhetoric is the same
rhetoric that was assigned to gay men ten

00:11:01.829 --> 00:11:06.530
years ago that has just moved on now and taken
a little bit of a different tone.

00:11:06.530 --> 00:11:13.820
But do you think that from a media studies
perspective, that it is a logical thing for

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:20.959
the right to be doing temporarily or even
on the medium term, or is it a mistake strategically?

00:11:20.959 --> 00:11:27.380
Speaker 5: It all depends on if, you know,
Murdoch has always been very good at taking

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:33.959
something that seems, you know, somewhat petty
like Eminem's be having, you know, you know,

00:11:33.959 --> 00:11:36.070
androgynous boots on or something like that.

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:39.230
And everyone that laughs, the left laughs
at it, right?

00:11:39.230 --> 00:11:40.690
We're like, Oh, it's so ridiculous.

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:42.620
They care about these little things.

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:47.370
But it's the way in which they'll take something
that is palpable and mundane and everyday

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:50.370
and connect it to the broader world of politics.

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:53.779
And that actually makes politics more palpable,
gives it texture.

00:11:53.779 --> 00:11:59.650
And so they're only successful if they just
get too in the weeds with CETA, with CRT and

00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:03.959
trans issues, and don't connect it to these
broader narratives about how liberals look

00:12:03.959 --> 00:12:04.959
down on us.

00:12:04.959 --> 00:12:08.420
And they're part of the educated elite and
they're the campus based crowd and we're the

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:10.040
working class crowd.

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:14.610
That's when those kind of cultural war narratives
pack their biggest punch.

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:19.269
But it's like when I look at the Santas, for
example, he just lacks that ability to take

00:12:19.269 --> 00:12:24.829
those issues and kind of turn them into broader
class narratives about elitism and about we're

00:12:24.829 --> 00:12:29.660
on the side of authentic people and normal
people, and he's just overly on line.

00:12:29.660 --> 00:12:36.050
And it just seems like it starts to seem like
you're actually just you're your tactic is

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:37.050
to make it.

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:38.050
You're just scapegoating.

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:41.410
You're creating a scapegoat for the sake of
creating a scapegoat.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:45.890
And it doesn't feel like it has this broader
populist message where Trump is very good

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:50.250
at exploiting those class cultural tensions
between the college educated and the non-college

00:12:50.250 --> 00:12:51.529
educated class.

00:12:51.529 --> 00:12:53.170
So I think that's where they are.

00:12:53.170 --> 00:12:58.920
The best is when they can package it in some
sort of larger populist class critique.

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:06.070
It's at its worst when it just as nakedly
bigoted and nakedly a kind of subculture of

00:13:06.070 --> 00:13:09.720
the right that they're obsessed with an extreme
kind of religious subculture.

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:16.141
Speaker 1: Speaking of like the online media
space versus kind of like outside of it, one

00:13:16.141 --> 00:13:21.660
of the conversations I had with my audience
during the 2020 Democratic primary was that

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:30.589
spending a lot of time on Twitter and Reddit
would likely give you an overemphasized perspective

00:13:30.589 --> 00:13:33.870
on how popular Bernie Sanders is within the
Democratic Party.

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:38.170
And I had the belief, and I think there was
polling to bear it out, that people who are

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:43.740
just like working in Michigan and then picking
up their kids and going home, they're not

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:49.760
necessarily on Twitter and Reddit in the same
way if they're on it at all, and that Joe

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:55.260
Biden's support among the Democratic Party
voters is likely much higher than it seems.

00:13:55.260 --> 00:13:56.930
And this seemed to be the case now.

00:13:56.930 --> 00:14:00.350
Yes, to some degree, there was this coordinated.

00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:03.380
Everybody got out of Joe Biden's way after
South Carolina.

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:04.380
That's that's true.

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:07.320
But I think the point kind of still remains
there.

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:15.720
Has this online discourse actually become
deceptive in terms of figuring out what, quote,

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:19.480
what people care about, maybe not only on
the left, but also on the right in the sense

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:24.290
that I don't know that that many Republican
voters, the sorts of people that kind of want

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:29.170
like a Chris Christie or a mitt Romney are
that engaged with like the so-called men and

00:14:29.170 --> 00:14:34.630
women's sports thing or the anti-woke has
online really distorted what people care about.

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:40.670
Speaker 5: I mean, I think undeniably, you
know, I think that what when you really get

00:14:40.670 --> 00:14:45.139
to the 2016 election, which is really this
important, pivotal shift, I think, in our

00:14:45.139 --> 00:14:50.029
media culture and our politics, where, you
know, you're no longer, you know, seen Obama

00:14:50.029 --> 00:14:55.220
dance with Ellen DeGeneres and Hillary Clinton,
you know, goes on stage with Beyonce.

00:14:55.220 --> 00:14:58.600
It's like this is that old kind of monoculture
model, right?

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:02.670
The vestiges of the network era where you
could still do that kind of politics.

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:08.480
What we see in 2016 is the online fringes
start to emerge and have outsized influence,

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:14.339
kind of erasing this normie kind of middlebrow
political center.

00:15:14.339 --> 00:15:15.339
Right.

00:15:15.339 --> 00:15:20.259
And so it's it creates this perception that
they're bigger or they're more representative

00:15:20.259 --> 00:15:24.220
of both their respective political sides than
they actually are.

00:15:24.220 --> 00:15:25.329
And I think you're right.

00:15:25.329 --> 00:15:30.199
I kind of was guilty of that, too, when you
when you consumed Bernie kind of aligned media

00:15:30.199 --> 00:15:32.910
so much, you know, when the 2020 election
happened.

